==English==

===Etymology===
{{bor|en|la|dēterreō||deter, discourage}}, from {{m|la|de||from}} + {{m|la|terreō||I frighten}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/dɪˈtɜː(ɹ)/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-deter.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɜː(r)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|deterr}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[prevent]] something from happening.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[persuade]] someone not to do something; to [[discourage]].
#* '''1748'''. David Hume. ''Enquiries concerning the human understanding and concerning the principles of moral.'' London: Oxford University Press, 1973. § 10.
#*: we have in following enquiry, attempted to throw some light upon subjects, from which uncertainty has hitherto '''deterred''' the wise

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|To persuade someone to not do something}} {{l|en|dissuade}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|deterrent}}

